.TH CLRUCACHE_CAPACITY 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
clrucache_capacity \- return the maximum capacity of the cache
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clrucache.h>

size_t clrucache_capacity(clru_cache cache);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns the maximum number of live entries that the cache can hold
before eviction starts. This is the value that the caller gives as
capacity to clrucache_create_full() at construction time.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cache  Cache handle.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
The capacity, which is always >= 1 for a valid handle. It is 0 when
cache is CLRU_CACHE_INVALID, and 0 when cache is a stale handle that a
call already destroyed.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
cache is an opaque VALUE handle. The library resolves it through a slot
table that the library owns, before it touches the cache object behind
the handle. A stale handle here is an ordinary error, and not a fatal
one. Only clru_destroy() treats a stale handle as fatal.
This function takes no lock. The creation of the cache fixes the
capacity, and nothing writes it again. The capacity is the total across
the segments of the cache. That total is exactly the value that the
caller gives to the constructor.
The complexity is O(1).
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clrucache_size (3),
.BR clrucache_create_full (3)
